.\"                                      Hey, EMACS: -*- nroff -*-
.TH TCPSER 1 "November 2026" "tcpser 1.4.1"
.SH NAME
tcpser \- Hayes compatible modem emulator over TCP/IP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tcpser
\-d \fIdevice\fP [\-s \fIspeed\fP] [\fIoptions\fP] ...
.br
.B tcpser
\-v \fIport\fP [\-s \fIspeed\fP] [\fIoptions\fP] ...
.br
.B tcpser
\-d \fIdevice\fP \-Z
.br
.B tcpser
\-\-config \fIfile\fP [\fIoverrides\fP]
.br
.B tcpser check
[\fIsame options\fP]
.br
.B tcpser help
[config | markdown | man]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBtcpser\fP turns a PC serial port into an emulated Hayes compatible modem that
uses TCP/IP for incoming and outgoing connections.  It can be used to allow
older applications and systems designed for modem use to operate on the
Internet.  Designed for hosting Commodore 64 BBS systems with SwiftLink,
Turbo232, GLINK232, and userport RS232 adapters.
.PP
This hardened fork adds a security gate (bot protection), persistent IP
blocklist, PETSCII pre-connect screens, and an RS232 analyzer mode for
diagnosing adapter wiring.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option has a short flag, a long flag and a configuration-file key of the
same name. Values are validated at startup; a bad value prints a message and
exits with status 2.
.\" options:begin
.SS "General"
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fP \fIFILE\fP
Read options from a config file (\fBkey = value\fP, one per line, \fB[line]\fP sections for each modem; \fBtcpser help config\fP prints a template). Flags given on the command line are applied after the file.
.TP
\fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP
Help. \fBtcpser help config\fP prints a config template, \fBhelp markdown\fP / \fBhelp man\fP the option tables.
.TP
\fB\-V\fP, \fB\-\-version\fP
Print the version and exit.
.SS "Serial"
.TP
\fB\-d\fP, \fB\-\-device\fP \fIDEVICE\fP
Serial device, e.g. \fB/dev/ttyUSB0\fP. Repeat (or mix with \fB\-v\fP) for a multi\-line BBS; each \fB\-d\fP/\fB\-v\fP starts a new line.
.TP
\fB\-v\fP, \fB\-\-ip232\fP \fIPORT\fP
Virtual RS232 over TCP (ip232 mode, e.g. VICE). Binds \fB127.0.0.1\fP; set \fBTCPSER_IP232_BIND\fP / \fB\-\-ip232\-bind\fP to expose it (whoever reaches this port controls the modem, so firewall it).
.TP
\fB\-s\fP, \fB\-\-speed\fP \fISPEED\fP
Serial port speed (default 38400; standard rates 300–230400).
.TP
\fB\-S\fP, \fB\-\-connect\-speed\fP \fISPEED\fP
Speed reported in the \fBCONNECT\fP message (default = \fB\-\-speed\fP).
.TP
\fB\-i\fP, \fB\-\-init\fP \fISTRING\fP
Modem init string without \fBAT\fP, e.g. \fB\-i "&C0&K3S0=1"\fP. \fBATZ\fP and \fBAT&F\fP restore it.
.TP
\fB\-I\fP, \fB\-\-invert\-dcd\fP
Invert the DCD line.
.TP
\fB\-b\fP, \fB\-\-baud\-menu\fP \fILIST\fP
On\-connect caller speed menu for CBase: \fBspeed=pokeval\fP pairs, e.g. \fB\-b 2400=6,9600=8,38400=10\fP. The caller picks a speed, tcpser sends \fBPOKE 838,pokeval\fP to CBase and retunes the serial port.
.SS "Network"
.TP
\fB\-p\fP, \fB\-\-listen\fP \fIPORT[:MODEMS]\fP
TCP listen port (default 6400, all modems). Repeatable; \fB\-p 23:0,1 \-p 64128:2\fP routes ports to specific lines.
.TP
\fB\-n\fP, \fB\-\-phonebook\fP \fINUM=HOST:PORT\fP
Phone book entry for \fBATD<number>\fP, e.g. \fB\-n 5551212=bbs.example.com:23\fP. Repeatable.
.TP
\fB\-D\fP, \fB\-\-direct\fP \fIHOST:PORT\fP
Direct connection to \fBHOST:PORT\fP (\fB:\fP alone = receiver mode).
.SS "Security / bot gate"
.TP
\fB\-x\fP, \fB\-\-backspace\-gate\fP
Require BACKSPACE twice before the modem rings (bots never send it).
.TP
\fB\-k\fP, \fB\-\-password\fP \fIPASSWORD\fP
Require a password before the modem rings. \fB\-k env:VAR\fP reads it from the environment so it never shows in \fBps\fP.
.TP
\fB\-W\fP, \fB\-\-gate\-timeout\fP \fISECONDS\fP
Gate timeout in seconds (default 20, max 300).
.TP
\fB\-F\fP, \fB\-\-gate\-fails\fP \fICOUNT\fP
Gate failures before the caller's IP is blocked permanently (default 3, range 1–100).
.TP
\fB\-P\fP, \fB\-\-banner\fP \fIFILE|DIR\fP
Pre\-connect PETSCII banner; a directory rotates banners randomly.
.TP
\fB\-G\fP, \fB\-\-granted\fP \fIFILE\fP
Access\-granted screen sent after the gate.
.TP
\fB\-E\fP, \fB\-\-gate\-hook\fP \fINAME\fP
Gate\-success hook: a script NAME (not a path) inside \fB/usr/local/tcpser/scripts/\fP; gets the caller IP in \fBTCPSER_PEER_IP\fP.
.SS "Event screens"
.TP
\fB\-a\fP, \fB\-\-answer\-local\fP \fIFILE\fP
Sent to the C64 on answer.
.TP
\fB\-A\fP, \fB\-\-answer\-remote\fP \fIFILE\fP
Sent to the caller on answer.
.TP
\fB\-c\fP, \fB\-\-connect\-local\fP \fIFILE\fP
Sent to the C64 on connect.
.TP
\fB\-C\fP, \fB\-\-connect\-remote\fP \fIFILE\fP
Sent to the caller on connect.
.TP
\fB\-N\fP, \fB\-\-no\-answer\fP \fIFILE\fP
Sent to the caller when nobody answers.
.TP
\fB\-B\fP, \fB\-\-busy\fP \fIFILE\fP
Sent to the caller when every line is busy.
.TP
\fB\-T\fP, \fB\-\-timeout\-screen\fP \fIFILE\fP
Sent to the caller on inactivity timeout.
.SS "Logging"
.TP
\fB\-l\fP, \fB\-\-log\-level\fP \fI0\-7\fP
Log level: 0 none, 1 fatal … 4 info … 7 trace (default 0).
.TP
\fB\-L\fP, \fB\-\-log\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP
Log file (SIGHUP reopens it for logrotate).
.TP
\fB\-t\fP, \fB\-\-trace\fP \fIFLAGS\fP
Hex traces: \fBm\fP/\fBM\fP modem in/out, \fBs\fP/\fBS\fP serial in/out, \fBi\fP/\fBI\fP IP in/out (lower = in, upper = out).
.TP
\fB\-O\fP, \fB\-\-connlog\fP \fIFILE\fP
Connection CSV log (created mode 0600).
.TP
\fB\-Y\fP, \fB\-\-stats\fP \fIFILE\fP
Persist counters across restarts (saved every 60 s and on exit).
.SS "Recovery / limits"
.TP
\fB\-w\fP, \fB\-\-watchdog\fP \fISECONDS\fP
BBS watchdog: toggle DTR if the BBS does not re\-arm after a disconnect (default 120 s, 0 = off, max 600).
.TP
\fB\-m\fP, \fB\-\-max\-session\fP \fIMINUTES\fP
Maximum session time in minutes (default 0 = unlimited).
.SS "Monitoring / notify"
.TP
\fB\-M\fP, \fB\-\-monitor\fP
Terminal dashboard on stderr (needs a tty). \fBTCPSER_TUI_PHOSPHOR\fP, \fBTCPSER_TUI_ASCII\fP and \fBNO_COLOR\fP tune it.
.TP
\fB\-H\fP, \fB\-\-web\fP \fIPORT\fP
Web console and Prometheus \fB/metrics\fP on \fBPORT\fP. Binds \fB127.0.0.1\fP unless \fB\-\-web\-bind\fP says otherwise.
.TP
\fB\-\-web\-bind\fP \fIADDR\fP
Bind address of the web console (default \fB127.0.0.1\fP; expose only behind nginx + TLS). Also the environment variable \fBTCPSER_DASH_BIND\fP.
.TP
\fB\-\-web\-token\fP \fITOKEN\fP
Require \fB?token=TOKEN\fP once to sign in to the web console (letters, digits, \fB\-\fP, \fB_\fP, \fB.\fP). Prefer the environment variable: a token on the command line is visible in \fBps\fP. Also the environment variable \fBTCPSER_DASH_TOKEN\fP.
.TP
\fB\-\-web\-traffic\fP
Include payload content in the web traffic feed (callers' keystrokes; keep it behind a token). Also the environment variable \fBTCPSER_DASH_TRAFFIC\fP.
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fP \fINAME\fP
BBS name shown in the web console header. Also the environment variable \fBTCPSER_DASH_NAME\fP.
.TP
\fB\-e\fP, \fB\-\-webhook\fP \fIURL\fP
Webhook URL for connection events (Discord, Slack, ntfy or plain JSON POST). Repeatable, up to 8.
.TP
\fB\-\-phosphor\fP \fIgreen|amber|white\fP
Terminal dashboard phosphor (\fBgreen\fP, \fBamber\fP or \fBwhite\fP). Also the environment variable \fBTCPSER_TUI_PHOSPHOR\fP.
.TP
\fB\-\-ascii\fP
Draw the terminal dashboard with ASCII glyphs even in a UTF\-8 locale. Also the environment variable \fBTCPSER_TUI_ASCII\fP.
.SS "Files"
.TP
\fB\-\-blocklist\fP \fIFILE\fP
Persistent IP blocklist (default \fBtcpser.blocklist\fP in the working directory). Also the environment variable \fBTCPSER_BLOCKLIST\fP.
.TP
\fB\-\-allowlist\fP \fIFILE\fP
Allowlist of IPs that skip the blocklist, the rate limiter and the gate (default \fBtcpser.allowlist\fP in the working directory). Also the environment variable \fBTCPSER_ALLOWLIST\fP.
.TP
\fB\-\-ip232\-bind\fP \fIADDR\fP
Bind address of the \fB\-v\fP (ip232) listener (default \fB127.0.0.1\fP). Also the environment variable \fBTCPSER_IP232_BIND\fP.
.TP
\fB\-Z\fP, \fB\-\-analyze\fP
RS232 analyzer: diagnose adapter wiring (needs \fB\-d\fP).
.\" options:end
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.B tcpser \-\-config
.I file
reads options from a file: one
.B key = value
per line, keys are the long option names, flags take yes/no, `#' starts a
comment. Everything before the first
.B [line]
section is global; each
.B [line]
section is one modem and must contain
.B device
or
.BR ip232 .
Per-line settings (speed, init, gate, screens, watchdog) carry over to the
next line unless changed. Flags given on the command line are applied after
the file and override it. Secrets belong in the environment
.RB ( "password = env:VAR" ,
.BR TCPSER_DASH_TOKEN ).
.PP
.B tcpser help config
prints a commented template;
.B tcpser check \-\-config
.I file
validates it, prints the resolved configuration and exits 0 (or 2 with the
offending line number) without opening ports or creating files.
.PP
.nf
listen     = 6400
log-level  = 4
log-file   = /var/log/tcpser/tcpser.log
web        = 8080

[line]
device         = /dev/ttyUSB0
speed          = 2400
backspace-gate = yes
banner         = /usr/local/share/tcpser/prelogin.seq
.fi
.SH NOTES
Options \-d/\-v can be repeated for multi-line BBS configurations.
Options \-s, \-S, \-i, and gate settings propagate to subsequent modems.
The option tables above are generated from the binary (\fBmake docs\fP).
.PP
DCD follows carrier by default (AT&C1).  6551 ACIA-based cartridges
(SwiftLink, Turbo232, GLINK232) disable their receiver when DCD is inactive;
if your BBS needs DCD asserted while idle, use \-i &C0 (or \-I to invert the
line).  ATZ and AT&F restore the settings given with \-i and \-I, not factory
defaults.
.SH EXAMPLES
Basic BBS hosting:
.PP
.nf
tcpser -d /dev/ttyUSB0 -s 2400 -p 6400 -x
.fi
.PP
With password gate and custom screens:
.PP
.nf
tcpser -d /dev/ttyUSB0 -s 2400 -p 6400 -k "secret" \\
  -P data/prelogin.seq -G data/accessgranted.seq
.fi
.PP
Analyzer mode:
.PP
.nf
tcpser -d /dev/ttyUSB0 -s 2400 -Z
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/tcpser/tcpser.conf
Configuration file used by the shipped systemd unit (see CONFIGURATION FILE).
.TP
.I data/tcpser.blocklist
Default persistent IP blocklist.  Override with TCPSER_BLOCKLIST
environment variable.
.TP
.I data/tcpser.allowlist
Trusted IPs that skip the blocklist, rate limiter and gate.  Override
with TCPSER_ALLOWLIST.
.TP
.I data/*.seq
PETSCII screen files for pre-connect banner, access granted, busy,
and backspace gate screens.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TCPSER_BLOCKLIST
Path to the persistent IP blocklist file.
.TP
.B TCPSER_ALLOWLIST
Path to the IP allowlist file.
.TP
.B TCPSER_DASH_BIND
Bind address for the \fB\-H\fP web dashboard (default 127.0.0.1).  Set to
0.0.0.0 to expose it on all interfaces.
.TP
.B TCPSER_DASH_TOKEN
If set, the \fB\-H\fP web dashboard requires \fB?token=<value>\fP on every
request (constant-time comparison).
.TP
.B TCPSER_DASH_TRAFFIC
If set to 1, the \fB\-H\fP web dashboard's live traffic feed includes an ASCII
preview of payload CONTENT (otherwise only direction and byte counts are
shown).  Payloads can contain what callers type into the BBS, so only enable
this behind localhost and a token.
.TP
.B TCPSER_PEER_IP
Set by tcpser in the environment of the \fB\-E\fP gate-success script; holds
the caller's IP address.
